---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../utils/i18n';

const lang = 'en';
const t = useTranslations(lang);
---

<Layout 
  title="Active Coping Guide | Health Tools" 
  description="Practical and interactive self-management tools for pain and gradual exercise to regain your autonomy, based on scientific evidence." 
  lang={lang} 
  canonicalPath="/en/coping-guide"
>
  <div class="article-detail" style="max-width: 900px;">
    <span class="hero-subtitle">{t('nav.guide')}</span>
    <h1 class="hero-title" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
      Active Coping Guide
    </h1>
    <p class="hero-description" style="margin-bottom: 40px;">
      Persistent pain can make us feel fragile and limit our activities. These evidence-based tools will help you train your body and nervous system to regain your active life, step by step.
    </p>

    <!-- PIPT Toolkit sections -->
    <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 40px;">
      
      <!-- Section 1: Graded Activity Calculator -->
      <section class="contact-card" aria-labelledby="graded-heading" style="padding: 30px;">
        <div class="card-image-wrapper" style="height: 220px;">
          <img src="/images/.webp" alt="Person engaged in outdoors graded activity and walking exercise" class="card-image" />
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px; margin-bottom: 15px;">
          <span style="font-size: 2rem;" aria-hidden="true">📊</span>
          <h2 id="graded-heading" style="font-size: 1.4rem; margin: 0;">
            1. Graded Activity Planner (Quota-based Movement)
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 25px;">
          To overcome kinesiophobia (fear of movement), science proposes exercises based on <strong>fixed time quotas</strong>, independent of immediate pain levels. Use this planner to define your safe starting point.
        </p>

        <!-- Interactive Calculator Form -->
        <div style="background-color: var(--bg-color); border: 1px solid var(--border-color); padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px;">
          <div class="form-group">
            <label for="activity-name" class="form-label">What activity do you want to recover? (e.g. Walking, Sitting at office desk, Carrying weight)</label>
            <input type="text" id="activity-name" placeholder="Type activity here..." class="form-control" />
          </div>

          <div class="form-group">
            <label for="max-time" class="form-label">How many minutes can you perform this activity on your worst day before pain becomes unbearable?</label>
            <input type="number" id="max-time" min="1" placeholder="e.g. 10" class="form-control" />
          </div>

          <button id="calc-btn" class="btn-submit" style="margin-top: 10px; width: auto; padding: 12px 25px;">
            Generate Movement Plan
          </button>
        </div>

        <!-- Calculator Result Area -->
        <div id="calc-result" style="display: none; margin-top: 25px; padding: 20px; background-color: rgba(51, 99, 103, 0.05); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-secondary); border-radius: 4px;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; margin-bottom: 10px; color: var(--color-secondary);">Your Personalized Plan</h3>
          <p id="result-text" style="font-size: 0.95rem; line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 15px;"></p>
          <div style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-muted);">
            <strong>Scientific Note:</strong> By completing exactly the set quota (no more, no less), your brain learns to uncouple movement from danger signals, strengthening your prefrontal cortex.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Pacing Guide -->
      <section class="contact-card" aria-labelledby="pacing-heading" style="padding: 30px;">
        <div class="card-image-wrapper" style="height: 220px;">
          <img src="/images/.webp" alt="Person pacing activity and taking balanced restful breaks" class="card-image" />
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px; margin-bottom: 15px;">
          <span style="font-size: 2rem;" aria-hidden="true">⏱️</span>
          <h2 id="pacing-heading" style="font-size: 1.4rem; margin: 0;">
            2. Pacing (Activity Regulation)
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
          The biggest enemy of recovery is the "Boom and Bust" cycle (doing too much on good days and being bedridden on bad days). Pacing teaches you to break activities into manageable blocks.
        </p>
        
        <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 20px; margin-top: 15px;">
          <div style="padding: 15px; background: rgba(220, 53, 69, 0.05); border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #dc3545;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #dc3545; margin-bottom: 8px;">What to Avoid (Boom & Bust)</h3>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-color); margin: 0;">
              <li>Cleaning the whole house at once because "it doesn't hurt as much today".</li>
              <li>Ignoring early warning signs and pushing your body to exhaustion.</li>
              <li>Complete bed rest for days after a pain flare-up.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div style="padding: 15px; background: rgba(40, 167, 69, 0.05); border-radius: 6px; border-left: 3px solid #28a745;">
            <h3 style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #28a745; margin-bottom: 8px;">Recommended Approach (Active Pacing)</h3>
            <ul style="padding-left: 20px; font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--text-color); margin: 0;">
              <li>Divide large tasks into 15-20 minute blocks.</li>
              <li>Schedule brief active breaks <strong>before</strong> severe pain starts.</li>
              <li>Maintain a baseline level of activity every single day.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Prefrontal Brake (PNE & Relaxation) -->
      <section class="contact-card" aria-labelledby="brake-heading" style="padding: 30px;">
        <div class="card-image-wrapper" style="height: 220px;">
          <img src="/images/.webp" alt="Person practicing diaphragmatic breathing for vagal pain modulation" class="card-image" />
        </div>
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px; margin-bottom: 15px;">
          <span style="font-size: 2rem;" aria-hidden="true">🧠</span>
          <h2 id="brake-heading" style="font-size: 1.4rem; margin: 0;">
            3. The Prefrontal Brake (Modulation & Relaxation)
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.95rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
          Pain education and cognitive techniques are not just ideas; they physically modify nerve signals from your prefrontal cortex to your spinal cord, acting as a "handbrake" for pain.
        </p>

        <div style="border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color); padding-top: 20px;">
          <h3 style="font-size: 1rem; margin-bottom: 10px;">Coherent Diaphragmatic Breathing Exercise</h3>
          <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--text-muted); line-height: 1.6; margin-bottom: 15px;">
            This exercise stimulates the vagus nerve and reduces the alarm state of the brain's amygdala, helping to decrease pain hypersensitivity:
          </p>
          <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; padding-left: 20px; line-height: 1.6;">
            <li>Sit or lie down in a comfortable position. Place one hand on your chest and the other on your abdomen.</li>
            <li>Inhale slowly through your nose for 5 seconds, feeling your abdomen expand (not your chest).</li>
            <li>Exhale gently through your mouth for 5 seconds.</li>
            <li>Repeat this cycle for 3 to 5 minutes whenever you feel an increase in pain or tension.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Interactive script for calculator -->
  <script is:inline>
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
      const calcBtn = document.getElementById('calc-btn');
      const activityInput = document.getElementById('activity-name');
      const timeInput = document.getElementById('max-time');
      const resultDiv = document.getElementById('calc-result');
      const resultText = document.getElementById('result-text');

      if (calcBtn) {
        calcBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
          const activity = activityInput.value.trim() || 'your activity';
          const maxTime = parseInt(timeInput.value);

          if (!maxTime || maxTime <= 0) {
            alert('Please enter a valid number of minutes.');
            return;
          }

          const baseline = Math.max(1, Math.round(maxTime * 0.8));
          const week2 = Math.round(baseline * 1.25);
          const week3 = Math.round(baseline * 1.5);

          resultText.innerHTML = `
            To start retraining your body for the activity of <strong>"${activity}"</strong>:<br><br>
            • <strong>Week 1 (Your Quota):</strong> Perform the activity for exactly <strong>${baseline} minutes</strong> per day, no matter what. Stop when you reach that time, even if you feel strong enough to continue.<br>
            • <strong>Week 2:</strong> Increase the quota to <strong>${week2} minutes</strong> daily.<br>
            • <strong>Week 3:</strong> Increase the quota to <strong>${week3} minutes</strong> daily.<br><br>
            If you have a pain flare-up one day, <strong>do not give up</strong>. Complete your quota of ${baseline} minutes at a slower pace or with fractional rests, but complete the time. This teaches your brain that pain does not mean damage.
          `;
          
          resultDiv.style.display = 'block';
          resultDiv.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth' });
        });
      }
    });
  </script>
</Layout>
